course: dictionary
lesson: 12763
title: Earliest: the first in time
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Earliest» tells you which one comes first in time. It is the biggest form of «early», so you can compare three things: early, earlier, earliest. «The earliest train leaves at six o'clock.» That means no other train leaves before it.
explain: The earliest time is the soonest possible time. «We can visit on Tuesday at the earliest.» Here «at the earliest» means not before Tuesday, and possibly later. Try swapping in «the first possible time» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In history you will read about «the earliest humans» and «the earliest writing». These mean the first humans and the first writing that people made, the ones that came before all the others.
fact: «Earliest» is the superlative of «early»: early, earlier, earliest.
fact: «Earliest» means the first one in time, or the one that comes before all the others.
fact: «The earliest time» means the soonest possible time.
fact: «At the earliest» means not before this time, and possibly later.
fact: In a timetable, the earliest train is the first train of the day.
exercise: «The earliest train leaves at six o'clock.» What does earliest mean here? | The slowest one | *The first one in time | The latest one | The most expensive one
exercise: «We can visit on Tuesday at the earliest.» What does «at the earliest» mean? | *Not before Tuesday, and possibly later | Only on Sunday | Exactly on Tuesday and no other day | Before anyone else arrives
exercise: Which word is the superlative form of «early»? | earlier | lately | *earliest | early
test: «Dad said the earliest we can leave is seven o'clock.» What is the soonest we can leave? | *Seven o'clock | Eight o'clock | Noon | Six o'clock
test: «Historians study the earliest towns that people built.» What does earliest mean in this sentence? | The newest towns | *The first towns in time | The biggest towns | The towns with the most people
exam: Which phrase means not before a time, and possibly later? | from my earliest memory | earliest times | *at the earliest | the earliest possible
